TEXT: In a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) transformer layer, the routing mechanism is designed to activate only a sparse subset of parameters for each input token, significantly enhancing computational efficiency. When a token enters an MoE layer, it first passes through a lightweight gating network, typically a small linear projection followed by a softmax function. This gating network calculates a probability score for every available expert in the layer, indicating how relevant each expert is to the specific input token.

Instead of processing the token through all experts, the system selects only the top-k experts with the highest scores. For instance, if k=2, the token is routed exclusively to these two experts, while the remaining experts remain inactive for that specific token. The outputs from the selected experts are then weighted by their gating scores and summed to produce the final output for that token. This sparse activation pattern means that while the total number of parameters in an MoE model can be vastly larger than a dense model, the active computation per token remains constant and low.

This sparsity makes offloading experts to CPU RAM highly practical. Since only a small fraction of experts are active at any given time, the GPU does not need to hold the entire model’s weights in its high-speed VRAM simultaneously. Instead, the GPU can keep only the currently active experts in VRAM for fast processing, while the vast majority of inactive experts reside in the larger, slower CPU RAM. When a new token requires different experts, the system swaps the
